Point 1 (list a local pool in the last-resort fallback?) -- NOT built, a decision for the maintainer.
Facts: apply's worker_ocpnodes makes nodes // 1 OCPNodes <pool>-N for a local pool, providerRef local-default, no host. ensure_provider_cr writes no local-default CR, so apply never reconciles a local pool unless someone ran `ocp provider add --type local --name local-default`. validate() accepts a local pool beside a local CP (the same machine as the RKE2 server) and nodes > 1 on a local pool. The Rex::Rancher uninstall on 127.0.0.1 removes every RKE2/K3s install there, server and agent alike, and is idempotent. With the API up today a local worker's OCPNode goes first and takes a local CP's RKE2 down through the worker path, then the CP's uninstall runs again (t/77 pins the two 127.0.0.1 uninstalls). The CP fallback exists for "nothing recorded" (k116/k146) and runs only when no node was found at all.
Options:
(A) Leave it as k341 left it. Never a wrong uninstall. Beside a local CP nothing is lost (the CP's uninstall clears the machine). With a remote CP, a local pool and the API down, this machine keeps its agent and nothing says so.
(A') A plus one STDERR line when the API is down and ocp.yaml has a local pool: its worker (this machine) is found by its OCPNode only and is NOT torn down; run rke2-uninstall.sh here. Small, names what is left, deletes nothing on a guess.
(B) List a local pool in the last resort only. Almost never helps (a remote CP is usually recorded or labelled; a local CP already clears the machine) and adds a second uninstall.
(C) List a local pool from ocp.yaml on every run, one entry <pool>-1 that merges with its OCPNode. Uninstalls this machine with the API down, but: a non-empty node list suppresses the CP fallback (an ssh CP only ocp.yaml records would then be skipped); a pool apply never reconciled (its default, no local-default CR) still wipes whatever RKE2/K3s this machine runs, another project's cluster included; run from another machine or inside the Docker image it hits the wrong target or nothing. It brings back the guess-uninstall on 127.0.0.1 that k341 removed.
(D) Upstream, other lanes (choices/state/apply): validate() refuses a local pool beside a local CP and nodes > 1 on a local pool; apply writes a local-default CR or refuses a local pool.
Recommendation: A or A'. Not C. A' and D would be new cards; the dispatcher files them if wanted.

